    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters September 1st with a lineup headlined by **Josh Naylor**, who carries our highest efficiency score of 78.46 against the vulnerable Paez. The combination of an elite matchup (9.12 xERA) and the favorable park shift makes **Josh Naylor** our must-start anchor, closely followed by **Mickey Moniak** (75.75). **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% park boost and a soft matchup against Bradish, making him a prime candidate to capitalize on his elite sprint speed despite a slight negative adjustment for Bradish\u2019s hold-runner capability. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (75.26) also provides a high-floor start, capitalizing on the right-handed platoon advantage against Phillips to solidify our middle-infield and corner production.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Isaac Paredes**, who was edged out of the starting nine. While **Isaac Paredes** (55.38) provided a decent floor, his score was suppressed by the SP Skill of the opposing pitcher and a lack of elite speed compared to our active lineup. We prioritized the high-upside matchup for **Griffin Conine** (61.48), whose platoon advantage against the right-handed Dobnak offered a higher ceiling than the more stagnant projection for **Isaac Paredes**. We remain wary of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll**, both of whom are facing the high-level stuff of Luzardo. While **Corbin Carroll** remains in the lineup due to his undeniable speed threat, the SP Skill penalty for Luzardo drags his efficiency down to 52.77, placing him just outside our \"Strong\" tier.\n\nBeyond the specific matchups, we are monitoring the SB volatility for **Brice Turang** and **Mickey Moniak**. While **Brice Turang** maintains a solid 62.06, his score is slightly tempered by the game-planning of Boyd, who poses a moderate threat to our stolen base accumulation. Managers should note that **Edwin Arroyo** remains at the bottom of the order, and while his switch-hitting provides a slight cushion, his 51.85 score serves as a reminder that we are essentially chasing peripheral contributions from the bottom of our roster today. The rest of the bench\u2014including **Travis Bazzana**, **Max Muncy**, and **Trevor Story**\u2014remains locked out to preserve our precious game caps, as none of their matchups provided the necessary efficiency threshold to displace our projected starters.",
